03. PRIORITY TWO — RESTORE DRIFTER COLLISION
============================================================

CURRENT USER COMPLAINT:

The jellyfish-like Drifter no longer glitches its movement,
but Nima now falls through it when attempting to land.

The previous movement fix has created a collision problem.

Relevant files:

src/game/entities/creatures/Drifter.ts

src/game/entities/player/Player.ts

src/game/scenes/GameplayScene.ts

CURRENT IMPLEMENTATION:

The Drifter uses a kinematic Arcade Physics body.

Its movement is controlled by:

- body.moves = false.
- sprite.setPosition().
- body.updateFromGameObject().

It uses a small collision body:

116 x 22 pixels.

Its body is offset from the visual sprite.

It also has collision directions disabled to create
top-only platform behavior.

Do not assume that the new implementation is correct
merely because the movement is smooth.

IMPORTANT:

A moving platform must be both:

A. Visually moving.

B. Physically landable.

The current version may satisfy A while failing B.

ROOT-CAUSE INVESTIGATION:

Reproduce the problem in the actual game.

Inspect:

- Arcade Physics body position.
- Visible sprite position.
- Body size.
- Body offset.
- Body top coordinate.
- Visible moss-cap top coordinate.
- Collision direction flags.
- Body enabled state.
- updateFromGameObject timing.
- Collider process callbacks.
- Physics update order.
- Passenger-carry timing.
- Player grounded-state handling.

Do not assume body.moves = false alone disables collision.

Verify the correct semantics for the actual Phaser
3.87 implementation.

Do not blindly change the flag without testing.

REQUIRED COLLISION:

The Drifter must work as a reliable one-way moving platform.

Nima must be able to:

- Land on the top.
- Stand on the top.
- Remain standing while it moves.
- Walk on the platform.
- Jump off normally.
- Pass through the underside while jumping upward.
- Avoid unwanted side collisions.
